---
const title = "Sapporta Grid";
const description =
  "How Sapporta Grid separates its runtime, column presets, and table-aware layer.";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta
      name="viewport"
      content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"
    />
    <meta
      name="description"
      content={description}
    />
    <link
      rel="canonical"
      href="https://sapporta.com/grid/"
    />
    <link
      rel="icon"
      href="/favicon.svg"
      type="image/svg+xml"
    />
    <title>{title}</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <main>
      <article>
        <h1>Sapporta Grid</h1>

        <p>
          A data grid has two kinds of work to do.
          It must decide what sorting, selection,
          editing, and hierarchy mean, and it must
          put those decisions on a screen.
          Sapporta Grid keeps the two apart so
          that the behavior of a grid does not
          depend on the framework used to render
          it.
        </p>

        <p>
          To maintain that separation, all of
          those decisions belong to the core—to
          the grid runtime and, at the table-aware
          level, the grid session. The React
          components can then stay near the
          surface, observing the state, rendering
          it, and passing interactions back down.
          Because they do not carry the policy
          themselves, a Svelte or plain-JavaScript
          view could sit in their place and
          inherit the same behavior.
        </p>

        <p>
          That separation becomes the bottom
          layer, BaseGrid, which provides the
          runtime, the data-source contract, and a
          basic React view. Most grids need more
          than this bare foundation, so
          ColumnPreset builds on it with typed
          columns, editors, formatting, and
          copying. When the rows are registered
          Sapporta records, TGrid adds one more
          layer, connecting the same machinery to
          schema metadata, generated queries,
          lookups, saves, and record routes.
        </p>

        <p>
          Which layer you reach for depends less
          on how the grid should look than on
          where its rows come from. Registered
          Sapporta records usually remain in a
          generated table screen or TGrid, where
          the server still owns authorization and
          row security. By contrast, data
          assembled by the application—a temporary
          set of rows, a calculation, or a bounded
          projection for one screen—belongs in
          BaseGrid, generally with ColumnPreset.
        </p>

        <nav aria-label="Grid documentation">
          <a
            href="/grid/start/choose-a-grid-layer/"
            >Choose a layer</a
          >
          <a href="/grid/guides/core-model/"
            >Core model</a
          >
          <a
            href="/docs/guides/generated-surfaces/grid-first-record-workflows/"
          >
            Record workflows
          </a>
          <a
            href="/docs/reference/frontend/tgrid/"
            >TGrid reference</a
          >
        </nav>
      </article>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>

<style>
  :root {
    font-family:
      Inter,
      ui-sans-serif,
      system-ui,
      -apple-system,
      BlinkMacSystemFont,
      "Segoe UI",
      sans-serif;
    color: #20201e;
    background: #fafaf8;
    font-synthesis: none;
  }

  body {
    margin: 0;
  }

  main {
    box-sizing: border-box;
    display: grid;
    min-height: 100vh;
    min-height: 100svh;
    padding-block: clamp(1.5rem, 3vh, 3rem);
    padding-inline: clamp(2rem, 7vw, 6rem);
    place-items: center;
  }

  article {
    width: min(100%, 46rem);
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    font-size: 1rem;
    font-weight: 650;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }

  p {
    margin: 0 0 1.05rem;
    font-size: clamp(
      1rem,
      0.97rem + 0.2vw,
      1.125rem
    );
    letter-spacing: -0.012em;
    line-height: 1.62;
  }

  nav {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.7rem 1.3rem;
    margin-top: 1.85rem;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }

  a {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
    text-underline-offset: 0.25em;
  }

  a:hover {
    color: #65655f;
  }

  a:focus-visible {
    border-radius: 2px;
    outline: 2px solid currentColor;
    outline-offset: 4px;
  }
</style>
